<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="lesson-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Lesson 02</title>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-2-describe-functions">
        <title>Describing Functions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>For each of the following functions, describe the following:</p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>Intervals on which the function is increasing. If none, write <em>none</em>
                            .</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Intervals on which the function is decreasing. If none, write <em>none</em>
                            .</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Intervals on which the function is constant. If none, write <em>none</em>
                            .</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The numbers at which the function has a relative maximum. What are the
                            relative maxima? If none, write <em>none</em>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The numbers at which the function has a relative minimum. What are the
                            relative minima? If none, write <em>none</em>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-describe-functions-1" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-1">
                    <caption>Function Graph 1</caption>
                    <image>
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane displaying a piecewise linear graph with solid
                                dots marking specific vertices. The graph consists of connected line
                                segments starting at a point in the second quadrant, zigzagging up
                                and down across the axes, and ending with a horizontal ray pointing
                                to the right in the first quadrant. Solid dots are located at
                                x-coordinates -8, -5, -2, 0, 2, 5, and 7.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                            label="prefig-piecewise-inc-dec-const">
                            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                                <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
                                    <grid hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" stroke="lightgray"
                                        spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))" />
                                    <axes hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)"
                                        vlabels="(-8,2,8)" clear-background="yes" />
                                    <line p1="(-8,6)" p2="(-5,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(-5,2)" p2="(-2,8)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(-2,8)" p2="(0,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(0,2)" p2="(2,-9)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(2,-9)" p2="(5,-5)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(5,-5)" p2="(7,1)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <line p1="(7,1)" p2="(10,1)" thickness="2" arrows="1"
                                        stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(-8,6)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(-5,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(-2,8)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(0,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(2,-9)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(5,-5)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(7,1)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Trace the graph from left to right, noting the <m>x</m>-coordinates where the
                    graph changes direction. The function is increasing when the <m>y</m>-values go
                    up, decreasing when they go down, and constant when the line is perfectly flat.
                    "Peaks" indicate relative maxima and "valleys" indicate relative minima.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>Increasing: <m>(-5, -2) \cup (2, 7)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Decreasing: <m>(-8, -5) \cup (-2, 2)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Constant: <m>(7, \infty)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative maximum: <m>8</m> at <m>x = -2</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative minima: <m>2</m> at <m>x = -5</m> and <m>-9</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>By analyzing the coordinates of the vertices on the given graph, we can determine
                    the function's behavior over its domain:</p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p><em>Increasing:</em> The <m>y</m>-values rise between <m>x = -5</m>
                                and <m>x = -2</m>, and again between <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = 7</m>.
                                Expressed as open intervals, the function is increasing on <m>(-5,
                                -2) \cup (2, 7)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><em>Decreasing:</em> The <m>y</m>-values fall starting from <m>x = -8</m>
                                to <m>x = -5</m>, and they also fall continuously from <m>x = -2</m>
                                down to <m>x = 2</m>. Expressed as open intervals, the function is
                                decreasing on <m>(-8, -5) \cup (-2, 2)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><em>Constant:</em> The graph becomes a horizontal line starting at <m>x
                                = 7</m> and continues to the right indefinitely (indicated by the
                                arrow on the ray). Thus, the interval is <m>(7, \infty)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><em>Relative Maxima:</em> The graph has a "peak" (a point higher than
                                those immediately surrounding it) at the coordinate <m>(-2, 8)</m>.
                                Therefore, the function has a relative maximum of <m>8</m> at <m>x =
                                -2</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><em>Relative Minima:</em> The graph has two "valleys" (points lower
                                than those immediately surrounding them) at the coordinates <m>(-5,
                                2)</m> and <m>(2, -9)</m>. Therefore, the function has relative
                                minima of <m>2</m> at <m>x = -5</m> and <m>-9</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-describe-functions-2" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-2">
                    <caption>Function Graph 2</caption>
                    <image>
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane displaying a continuous, bell-shaped curve. The
                                x-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with numeric labels at -6, -4,
                                -2, 2, 4, and 6. The y-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with
                                numeric labels at -2, 2, and 4. The curve has arrows on both the
                                left and right ends, indicating it continues indefinitely. From the
                                left, the curve rises, passing through a solid black dot at (-3, 1),
                                reaching a peak at a solid black dot on the y-axis at (0, 3). From
                                this peak, it falls, passing symmetrically through a third solid
                                black dot at (3, 1) and flattening out as it approaches the x-axis
                                to the right.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                            label="prefig-describe-functions-bell-curve">
                            <diagram dimensions="(600,300)" margins="10">
                                <definition>f(x)=3*exp(-0.122068*x^2)</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-6.5,-2.5,6.5,4.5)">
                                    <grid hticks="(-6,1,6)" vticks="(-2,1,4)" stroke="lightgray"
                                        spacings="((-6,1,6),(-2,1,4))" />
                                    <axes hticks="(-6,2,6)" vticks="(-2,2,4)" hlabels="(-6,2,6)"
                                        vlabels="(-2,2,4)" clear-background="yes" />

                                    <graph function="f" domain="(-6,6)" thickness="2" arrows="2"
                                        stroke="blue" />

                                    <point p="(-3,1)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(3,1)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Trace the curve from left to right. Note the <m>x</m>-interval where the curve
                    goes up (increasing) and where it goes down (decreasing). Identify the
                    coordinates of the highest point (peak) to find the relative maximum.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>Increasing: <m>(-\infty, 0)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Decreasing: <m>(0, \infty)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Constant: none</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative maximum: <m>3</m> at <m>x = 0</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative minimum: none</p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>By tracing the graph from left to right, we can determine the following
                    behaviors:</p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>The <m>y</m>-values rise as <m>x</m> increases up to <m>x = 0</m>.
                                Therefore, the function is increasing on the interval <m>(-\infty,
                                    0)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The <m>y</m>-values fall as <m>x</m> increases from <m>x = 0</m>
                                onward. Therefore, the function is decreasing on the interval <m>(0,
                                \infty)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>There are no completely flat, horizontal sections on the graph. The
                                intervals where the function is constant is <em>none</em>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The graph has a single peak ("hill") at the point <m>(0, 3)</m>. The
                                function has a relative maximum at <m>x = 0</m>, and the relative
                                maximum is <m>3</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The graph smoothly extends outward without forming any low "valley"
                                points. The relative minimum is <em>none</em>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-describe-functions-3" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-3">
                    <caption>Function Graph 3</caption>
                    <image>
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane displaying a piecewise graph composed of three
                                connected sections. The first section is a straight line starting at
                                a solid dot at (-8, 1) and descending to a solid dot at (-2, -2).
                                The second section is a curve that dips downwards from (-2, -2) to a
                                lowest point crossing the y-axis at (0, -8), then rises to a solid
                                dot at (2, -2). The third section is a curve that begins at (2, -2),
                                increases while passing through (3, -1) and the x-axis at (4, 0),
                                and continues upwards and rightwards indefinitely, terminating with
                                an arrowhead.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-lesson-2-graph-3">
                            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                                <definition>f1(x)=1.5*x^2-8</definition>
                                <definition>f2(x)=(x-3)^3-1</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
                                    <grid hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" stroke="lightgray"
                                        spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))" />
                                    <axes hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)"
                                        vlabels="(-8,2,8)" clear-background="yes" />

                                    <line p1="(-8,1)" p2="(-2,-2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <graph function="f1" domain="(-2,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                    <graph function="f2" domain="(2,5.22)" thickness="2" arrows="1"
                                        stroke="blue" />

                                    <point p="(-8,1)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(-2,-2)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(2,-2)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Read the graph from left to right, identifying where the <m>y</m>-values rise,
                    fall, or stay flat. Look for the highest and lowest points in specific regions.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>Increasing: <m>(0, \infty)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Decreasing: <m>(-8, 0)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Constant: none</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative maximum: none</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Relative minimum: <m>-8</m> at <m>x = 0</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>By tracing the graph from left to right, we analyze the behavior of the <m>y</m>-values
                    over the <m>x</m>-intervals:</p>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p><strong>Increasing:</strong> The <m>y</m>-values rise continuously
                                starting from the valley at <m>x = 0</m> and moving towards
                                infinity. Therefore, the interval of increase is <m>(0, \infty)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><strong>Decreasing:</strong> The graph falls from its starting point
                                at <m>x = -8</m> down to the lowest point at <m>x = 0</m>. The
                                interval of decrease is <m>(-8, 0)</m> (or <m>[-8, 0)</m> if
                                including the endpoint).</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><strong>Constant:</strong> There are no purely horizontal sections on
                                this graph, so the function is never constant. Answer: <em>none</em>
                                .</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><strong>Relative Maxima:</strong> There are no "peaks" (points where
                                the graph changes from increasing to decreasing) in the interior of
                                the domain. Answer: <em>none</em>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><strong>Relative Minima:</strong> The graph has a "valley" at the <m>
                                y</m>-intercept. The <m>y</m>-value transitions from decreasing to
                                increasing here, creating a relative minimum of <m>-8</m> at <m>x =
                                    0</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry">
        <title>Completing Symmetry</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Complete the graph so that it has the specified symmetry. Use the reference graph
                below:</p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-ref-graph">
                <caption>Reference Graph</caption>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-review">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry-1" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>Symmetry with respect to the <m>x</m>-axis</p>
            </statement>

            <hint>
                <p>Reflect the existing parts of the graph vertically across the horizontal axis
                    (flip upside down).</p>
            </hint>

            <answer>
                <p>Graph reflected across the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-ans-x-axis">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,-3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </answer>

            <solution>
                <p>To complete the <m>x</m>-axis symmetry, map every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the
                    given graph to <m>(x, -y)</m>.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(0, 3)</m> becomes <m>(0, -3)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(-2, 0)</m> remains <m>(-2, 0)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The ray through <m>(-4, -1)</m> reflects to pass through <m>(-4, 1)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>Plot the newly transformed points and draw the corresponding line segment and ray
                    alongside the original graph.</p>

                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-sol-x-axis">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,-3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </exercise>


        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry-2" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>Symmetry with respect to the <m>y</m>-axis</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Reflect the existing parts of the graph horizontally across the vertical axis
                    (flip left to right).</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Graph reflected across the <m>y</m>-axis.</p>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-ans-y-axis">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>To complete the <m>y</m>-axis symmetry, map every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the
                    given graph to <m>(-x, y)</m>.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(0, 3)</m> becomes <m>(0, 3)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(-2, 0)</m> becomes <m>(2, 0)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The ray through <m>(-4, -1)</m> reflects to pass through <m>(4, -1)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>Plot the newly transformed points and draw the corresponding line segment and ray
                    alongside the original graph.</p>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-sol-y-axis">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry-3" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>Symmetry with respect to the origin</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Reflect the graph across both axes, or rotate it 180 degrees around the origin (<m>
                    (0,0)</m>).</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Graph reflected across the origin.</p>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-ans-origin">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(0,-3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(5.5,1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>For every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the given graph, plot the corresponding point <m>(-x,
                    -y)</m> to create origin symmetry.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(0, 3)</m> becomes <m>(0, -3)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>(-2, 0)</m> becomes <m>(2, 0)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>The ray through <m>(-4, -1)</m> reflects to pass through <m>(4, 1)</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p>Plot the newly transformed points and draw the corresponding line segment and ray
                    alongside the original graph.</p>
                <image width="50%">
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-compsym-sol-origin">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-5.5,5.5,5.5)">
                                <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-5,1,5),(-5,1,5))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)"
                                    vlabels="(-5,1,5)" clear-background="yes" />

                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(0,3)" thickness="3" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,0)" p2="(-5.5,-1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />
                                <point p="(0,3)" fill="blue" stroke="blue" size="6" />

                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(0,-3)" thickness="3" stroke="orange" />
                                <line p1="(2,0)" p2="(5.5,1.75)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                    stroke="orange" />
                                <point p="(0,-3)" fill="orange" size="6" stroke="orange" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry-4" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>Symmetric, but not with respect to the <m>x</m>-axis, <m>y</m>-axis, or origin</p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Choose an arbitrary line (like <m>y = x</m>) or point other than the origin and
                    reflect the graph across it.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Answers will vary. Graph reflected across a custom line or point.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Solutions will vary. A common alternative symmetry is reflection across the line <m>y
                    = x</m>, where every point <m>(x, y)</m> becomes <m>(y, x)</m>. Alternatively,
                    reflect across a vertical or horizontal line other than the axes.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-2-even-odd-neither">
        <title>Even, Odd, or Neither</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Determine if each of the following is even, odd, or neither.</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-even-odd-neither-1" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>y=x^4+3x^2-1</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Substitute <m>-x</m> for <m>x</m> and see if you get the original function (even)
                    or its exact opposite (odd).</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Even</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^4 + 3x^2 - 1</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= (-x)^4 + 3(-x)^2 - 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= x^4 + 3x^2 - 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= f(x)</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Because <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>, the function is even.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-even-odd-neither-2" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x=y^3</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Solve for <m>y</m> to write it as a function of <m>x</m>, then test <m>f(-x)</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Odd</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>First, solve the equation for <m>y</m> to express it as a function <m>f(x)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>x \amp= y^3</mrow>
                        <mrow>y \amp= \sqrt[3]{x}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= \sqrt[3]{-x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= -\sqrt[3]{x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= -f(x)</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Because <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m>, the function is odd.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-even-odd-neither-3" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>x^2+y^3=36</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Solve for <m>y</m> to write it as a function of <m>x</m>, then test <m>f(-x)</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Even</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>First, solve the equation for <m>y</m> to express it as a function <m>f(x)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>x^2 + y^3 \amp= 36</mrow>
                        <mrow>y^3 \amp= 36 - x^2</mrow>
                        <mrow>y \amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - (-x)^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= f(x)</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Because <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>, the function is even.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-2-graph-piecewise">
        <title>Graphing Piecewise Functions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Graph the following functions, then list the domain and range.</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-graph-piecewise-1" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <me>
                        f(x) = \begin{cases}
                        x+5 \amp \text{if } -10 \leq x \lt -2 \\
                        x^2+2x+3 \amp \text{if } x \geq -2
                        \end{cases}
                    </me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Graph the linear piece between <m>x = -10</m> and <m>x = -2</m> (open circle at <m>
                    x=-2</m>). Graph the parabolic piece for <m>x \geq -2</m> (closed circle at <m>
                    x=-2</m>).</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Domain: <m>[-10, \infty)</m>, Range: <m>[-5, \infty)</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>The domain is given by combining the intervals of the pieces: <m>[-10, -2) \cup
                    [-2, \infty) = [-10, \infty)</m>.</p>
                <p>To find the range, evaluate the outputs of each piece:</p>
                <p>For <m>x+5</m> on <m>[-10, -2)</m>, the outputs range from <m>-10+5 = -5</m> up
                    to, but not including, <m>-2+5 = 3</m>. This gives a range segment of <m>[-5, 3)</m>
                    .</p>
                <p>For <m>x^2+2x+3</m> on <m>[-2, \infty)</m>, find the vertex. The <m>x</m>-coordinate
                    is <m>x = \frac{-b}{2a} = \frac{-2}{2(1)} = -1</m>. The <m>y</m>-coordinate is <m>(-1)^2
                    + 2(-1) + 3 = 2</m>. The parabola opens upward, so its minimum value is <m>2</m>.
                    This gives a range segment of <m>[2, \infty)</m>.</p>
                <p>Combining the range segments gives <m>[-5, 3) \cup [2, \infty) = [-5, \infty)</m>
                    .</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-graph-piecewise-2" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <me>
                        f(x) = \begin{cases}
                        x^2-1 \amp \text{if } x \leq 0 \\
                        2x-1 \amp \text{if } 0 \lt x \leq 5 \\
                        3 \amp \text{if } x \gt 5
                        \end{cases}
                    </me>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Graph the parabola for <m>x \leq 0</m>, the straight line for <m>x</m> between <m>
                    0</m> and <m>5</m>, and the horizontal line for <m>x \gt 5</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Domain: <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>, Range: <m>[-1, \infty)</m></p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>The domain includes all real numbers because the combined intervals cover <m>(-\infty,
                    0] \cup (0, 5] \cup (5, \infty) = (-\infty, \infty)</m>.</p>
                <p>To find the range, evaluate the outputs of each piece:</p>
                <p>For <m>x^2-1</m> on <m>(-\infty, 0]</m>, the minimum occurs at the vertex <m>(0,
                    -1)</m> and opens upward, giving a range of <m>[-1, \infty)</m>.</p>
                <p>For <m>2x-1</m> on <m>(0, 5]</m>, the line segment goes from <m>y = 2(0)-1 = -1</m>
                    (exclusive) to <m>y = 2(5)-1 = 9</m> (inclusive), giving a range of <m>(-1, 9]</m>
                    .</p>
                <p>For <m>3</m> on <m>(5, \infty)</m>, the output is just the constant value <m>3</m>
                    .</p>
                <p>Combining all range outputs: <m>[-1, \infty) \cup (-1, 9] \cup \{3\} = [-1,
                    \infty)</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-2-difference-quotient">
        <title>Evaluating Difference Quotients</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Evaluate the difference quotient, <m>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}</m>, for each of the
                functions.</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-difference-quotient-1" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=2x^2-x</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Substitute <m>(x+h)</m> into the function carefully, expand, and cancel terms.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>4x + 2h - 1</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>First, find the expression for <m>f(x+h)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x+h) \amp= 2(x+h)^2 - (x+h)</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 2(x^2 + 2xh + h^2) - x - h</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Now apply the difference quotient formula:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h) -
                            (2x^2 - x)}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h - 2x^2 + x}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{4xh + 2h^2 - h}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{h(4x + 2h - 1)}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 4x + 2h - 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-2-difference-quotient-2" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=x^3-4</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Remember that <m>(x+h)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>3x^2 + 3xh + h^2</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>First, find the expression for <m>f(x+h)</m>:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x+h) \amp= (x+h)^3 - 4</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= (x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3) - 4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Now apply the difference quotient formula:</p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4) -
                            (x^3 - 4)}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4 - x^3 + 4}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{h(3x^2 + 3xh + h^2)}{h}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 3x^2 + 3xh + h^2</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>
</worksheet>